# {{.Title}}

`{{.ID}}` contributes to the `{{.Registry}}` registry of the platform. `aiopsctl plugin new
{{.Registry}} {{.ID}} --lang go` wrote it: every method under `internal/` is a stub that
returns "not implemented" until it is written.

## Writing it

1. Run `aiopsctl plugin check --write ./`, which resolves the modules of the plugin into
   `go.mod` and `go.sum` and writes the generated half of `plugin.yaml` and the generated
   tests `zz_cycle_test.go` and `zz_tenants_test.go`, then `go mod tidy`, which adds the
   modules of the tests: the generated cycle test imports the cycle runner of the test
   kit.
2. Fill in the stubs under `internal/`. A client of an external API is built on the
   injected dialer, and its credentials come from `secrets`, never from a log or a fixture.
3. Record the responses of the external API under `fixtures/`: the tests replay them, so
   they pass with the network off.
4. Run `aiopsctl plugin check ./` until it passes. It prints one line per failed obligation
   of the `{{.Registry}}` suite, with the obligation id, and that list is the work list.

The check runs the tests of the plugin (T1) under `-race`, which needs cgo and a C
compiler on this machine.

## Config

The row config is the struct `Config` of `plugin.go`; its JSON Schema is derived from the
type and its tags, and `plugin check --write` writes it into `plugin.yaml`.

## Permissions on the vendor side

State here the permissions that the plugin needs on the system it calls, and the
versions of it that the fixtures were recorded against.

## The row

`bundle.yaml` inserts one row, disabled. Once the plugin is installed, enable it with:

    aiopsctl plugin enable {{.Name}}:{{.Name}}
